-- Q11241: Business Structures [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under 11 U.S.C. Section 547(b), a trustee may avoid transfers made within 90 days before filing that enabled the creditor to receive more than it would in a Chapter 7 liquidation. The $80,000 payment made 47 days before filing to one unsecured creditor while others received nothing is a classic preferential transfer recoverable for the estate.
Wrong (A): A debtor''s right to pay creditors does not prevent the trustee from avoiding preferential transfers under Section 547 once bankruptcy is filed.
Wrong (C): The lookback period for preferential transfers is 90 days (or one year for insiders), not 30 days; the 47-day gap falls well within the statutory window.
Wrong (D): Preference recovery requires return of the full transferred amount, not merely any excess over fair market value of goods received.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11241;

-- Q11242: Business Structures [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under 11 U.S.C. Section 548, the trustee may avoid fraudulent transfers made within two years before filing. A transfer of a $500,000 property for $1 while insolvent constitutes constructive fraud because the debtor received less than reasonably equivalent value. The trustee need not prove actual intent to defraud.
Wrong (A): The homestead exemption protects a debtor''s residence equity, not transfers of commercial property to family members for nominal consideration.
Wrong (B): Section 548 provides a two-year lookback period for fraudulent transfers, not 30 days; the January-to-March transfer is well within that window.
Wrong (D): Section 548 specifically grants the trustee power to avoid pre-petition transfers; the filing date does not shield earlier fraudulent conveyances.$EXPL$
